109-### US-03-R1-F01 — A Strapi restart puts the seed terms back over the editor's terms (major)
110-
111-**Evidence (review stack `us03-review-r1`, fresh volume):**
112-
113-1. Through the admin API, as an editor would in the Content Manager, the CO terms title was
114-   changed to "Términos y condiciones del seguro de auto – Colombia (versión 2027)" and published
115-   (update 200, publish 200). `GET /api/terms?filters[country][code][$eq]=CO` returned only that
116-   entry.
117-2. `docker compose -p us03-review-r1 restart strapi`. The log printed
118-   `Seed: created terms CO/Términos y condiciones del seguro de auto – Colombia`.
119-3. The same request now returned two published entries:
120-   `[('…Colombia', '2026-11-21T20:01:57.533Z'), ('…Colombia (versión 2027)', '2026-11-21T20:01:51.964Z')]`.
121-   `parseTerms` shows the most recent one, so the summary shows the **fictitious seed text**, not
122-   the editor's terms.
123-
124-The same happens when an editor deletes the seeded entry and publishes new terms with another title.
125-This is the normal way to replace terms, since the spec has no versioning (US-03-Q-12). Container
126-restarts happen on every `docker compose up` and every deploy. Fields and plans use technical
127-natural keys (`key`) that editors don't rename, and their duplicates don't win by publication date.
128-The terms use the editor-facing `title` as their key, and the newest published entry wins, so a
129-restart overrides business content.
130-
131-**Why major:** BR-07's promise ("managed by the business team from Strapi … reflected on the next
132-full page load") breaks after a routine restart. The person then sees, and accepts, terms that the
133-business has withdrawn. No data is deleted, so this is not a blocker.
134-
135-**Direction (not a fix):** §7.3 fixes the natural key, so the spec-writer and the human have to
136-decide this, not the strapi-developer alone. Options: seed a country's terms only when that country
137-has **no** terms document at all (any status), or keep a seed marker that editors don't edit. Add a
138-backend test: rename or delete the seeded entry, re-run the seed, and check that no new published
139-entry appears.
